(vector (map (:db/id -1)
  (:adr/id "adr-2608271900-murakumo-readiness-reports-a-dead-node-online")
  (:adr/title "murakumo の /ready が死んだノードを online と答える — 心拍が来なかったことと、来て正常だったことが同じ値になる")
  (:adr/status "accepted")
  (:adr/date "2026-08-27")
  (:adr/tags (vector "murakumo" "fleet" "readiness" "fleet-ci" "placement" "measurement"))
  (:adr/related (vector "adr-2608111721" "adr-2608136000" "adr-2608271450" "adr-2608221300"))
  (:adr/body "## What was measured

`api.murakumo.cloud/ready` reports the fleet healthy while one of its eleven
nodes has been dead for a day, and CI has been failing on that node for five.

    /ready        online 11/11,  offline [],  ok true,  fresh true
    tailscale     100.101.27.85  naphtali  offline, last seen 1d ago, rx 0
    ping          2 packets transmitted, 0 received, 100.0% packet loss
    fleet-ci log  ssh timeouts to naphtali on every tick since 2026-08-23

## The mechanism, proven rather than inferred

Two samples of `GET /nodes`, 21 seconds apart:

    naphtali  ts=1787815895132  load1=None  free-gb=None
    naphtali  ts=1787815916155  load1=None  free-gb=None

**The timestamp advances while the node reports no metrics at all.** In the
same response, ten of the eleven rows share a `ts` within 20 ms
(…862131–…862152) while `gad` carries its own — the signature of one collector
stamping every row with its own clock, not eleven nodes reporting
independently.

`readiness.cljc/live-node?` then sees a fresh `ts` and a stored
`:reachable true` and calls the node live. The namespace docstring states the
invariant this defeats, in its own words:

> A stored descriptor is inventory; only a recent hwmetrics observation may
> call a node online.

The row **is** inventory. It is wearing an observation's timestamp.

A second path fails differently: `GET /nodes/naphtali` returns
`ts: None, reachable: None, online: True` — echoing a stored `online` claim
that `public-node`'s own docstring says never to copy (*they were historically
static and outlived the processes they described*).

## Why this is expensive, not untidy

ADR-2608111721 gives placement authority to `murakumo.task.plan` over
`:fleet-inventory`. An inventory that calls a dead node live keeps placing work
on it. Measured on the tick running at 2026-08-27T07:02Z:

| | |
|---|---|
| ssh timeouts to naphtali, this tick alone | 66 |
| distinct dependencies shipped at a dead host | 33 |
| timeouts today | 1,490 |
| tick runtime with no receipt signed | 37+ min |

At ~20s per timeout that is roughly 22 minutes of one tick spent posting
packages to an address with nobody at it. The tick cannot finish, so it cannot
sign a receipt — **so nothing about CI can be verified at all while this
persists.** Five days of it, with the fleet's own health signal reporting
`ok: true` throughout.

## This is the day's recurring failure, at the top of the stack

2026-08-27 produced the same shape in six unrelated systems: a value that means
*I could not measure* returned identically to *I measured, and it is fine*.

| where | could-not-measure | looked like |
|---|---|---|
| hyakka ingest | 401 from a pinned, unserved model | a growing wiki (ADR-2608271450) |
| fleet-ci lock | `mkdir` failing on a full disk | `another tick holds the lock` |
| dashboard build | output written to `hermes_cli/web_dist` | `web/dist` never appearing |
| `npx --yes npm@11.17.0` | flag misparse | npm 10.2.2 reporting success |
| `du -ch \\| tail -1` | last xargs batch only | 25 GB instead of 179 GB |
| `stat -f '%z'` | GNU coreutils `-f` = filesystem status | 86,850 lines summing to 0.0 GB |
| **this** | **no hwmetric arrived** | **`online: true`** |

The last two were my own tools, mid-investigation, while writing about the
first four.

## Decision

**Not fixed in this session, deliberately.** The change lands in
`network-awai/local-murakumo`, a live shared Worker whose output placement
depends on, and getting it wrong in the other direction — marking live nodes
dead — idles the fleet. The owner should choose.

Two moves, and they are separable:

1. **Unblock CI now.** Mark naphtali down in whatever the collector reads, so
   placement stops choosing it. This does not touch semantics and makes (2)
   unhurried. It still writes to the inventory placement depends on, so it
   needs an explicit go.
2. **Fix the semantics.** A row nobody heard from must not be stamped: drop the
   `(:ts snapshot)` fallback in `observed-at`, stop echoing stored `online` in
   the single-node route, and let a silent node age out. `:reachable` must come
   from a measurement rather than from inventory.

## Resume

```bash
curl -sS https://api.murakumo.cloud/nodes | \\
  python3 -c 'import json,sys; [print(n[\"name\"], n.get(\"ts\"), n.get(\"load1\")) for n in json.load(sys.stdin)]'
tailscale status | grep naphtali
grep -c 100.101.27.85 ~/.gftd/fleet-ci-tick.log
```

Source: `orgs/network-awai/local-murakumo/src/local_murakumo/readiness.cljc`
(`observed-at`, `live-node?`, `public-node`, `fleet-summary`) and the
`/nodes/<id>` route in `routes.cljc`.

**Do not read a green `/ready` as evidence that the fleet can do work until
this is closed.** It currently answers a different question: whether a row was
written, not whether a machine answered.")))
